.TH NA_C_PLAY_TO_SPEAKERS 1 "2026-08-30" "naudio" "naudio manual"
.SH NAME
na_c_play_to_speakers \- play a naudio stream on the local speakers
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B na_c_play_to_speakers
.RB [ \-\-host
.IR H ]
.RB [ \-\-port
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-name
.IR S ]
.RB [ \-\-playback\-id
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-transport
.IR tcp | udp ]
.RB [ \-\-reliability
.IR lan | wan | ft8 ]
.RB [ \-\-seconds
.IR N ]
.RB [ \-\-backend
.IR system | null ]
.br
.B na_c_play_to_speakers \-\-list\-devices
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B na_c_play_to_speakers
connects to a naudio streaming server \(em
.BR na_audio_source (1),
.BR na_audio_daemon (1),
or any application serving the protocol \(em and plays whatever it is
streaming on this machine's speakers (or any output device).
With no options it connects to a server on the local machine and plays to
the default output: the listening half of the out-of-the-box demo.
.PP
It is also the reference client of the naudio examples suite \(em the same
program exists in C++, Python, Java, and Rust in the source tree, each
driving the identical public C API.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-host " H"
server to connect to (default 127.0.0.1).
.TP
.BI \-\-port " N"
server port (default 4533).
.TP
.BI \-\-name " S"
this client's name in the server's roster (default na-c-client).
.TP
.BI \-\-playback\-id " N"
output device to play on, by the id shown by
.B \-\-list\-devices
(default: the first output device).
.TP
.BI \-\-transport " tcp\fR|\fPudp"
network transport (default tcp) \(em must match the server's.
.TP
.BI \-\-reliability " lan\fR|\fPwan\fR|\fPft8"
loss-recovery profile for UDP.
UDP requires one \(em plain UDP with no recovery is refused.
.TP
.BI \-\-seconds " N"
run time in seconds; 0 runs until Ctrl-C (default 0).
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " system\fR|\fPnull"
.I system
(default) plays to a real output device;
.I null
receives the stream but plays nothing \(em useful to check a connection on
a machine with no sound output.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-devices
print the ids of output devices, then exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
print usage and exit.
.SH EXAMPLES
Listen to a server on the same machine:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_c_play_to_speakers
.RE
.fi
.PP
Listen to another machine on the network:
.PP
.nf
.RS
na_c_play_to_speakers \-\-host 192.168.1.20
.RE
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR na_audio_source (1),
.BR na_audio_daemon (1),
.BR na_wav_tap (1)
